Mastering DNS with AWS Route 53 📍
Topic: Global Domain Management & Traffic Routing with AWS Route 53

AWS Route 53 – the backbone of AWS’s highly available and scalable Domain Name System (DNS) service!

🔍 What is Route 53?
AWS Route 53 is a managed DNS service that lets you:

Register domain names 🌍

Route traffic to AWS and non-AWS resources 🚦

Monitor health and failover routing 🔁

Manage private DNS inside VPCs 🔒

📌 Core Features:
Domain Registration – Buy and manage domains directly via AWS

DNS Routing – Map domain names to AWS EC2, S3, ELB, CloudFront, etc.

Health Checks – Automatically route traffic away from unhealthy endpoints

Traffic Flow – Latency-based, weighted, failover, and geolocation routing

Private Hosted Zones – Manage internal domains inside your VPC securely

🧩 Routing Policies:

Policy Use Case
Simple Routing Single resource mapping
Weighted Routing A/B testing or load balancing
Latency-based Routing Route users to the lowest-latency endpoint
Failover Routing Backup website for disaster recovery
Geolocation Routing Region-specific content delivery
🧠 Bonus Tip:
Pair Route 53 with CloudFront CDN + S3 static website for blazing-fast, globally available websites! 🌎⚡

🌟 Why Route 53?
✅ Extremely reliable and globally available
✅ Natively integrates with AWS services
✅ Flexible for both small startups and global enterprises
✅ Built-in DDoS protection with AWS Shield Standard

